From: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
Subject: [PATCH] sdk_dpaa: let this netdev host a hardware qdisc

A hardware qdisc gives each leaf class a netdev queue index, and sch_htb
turns the index a driver returns straight into netdev_get_tx_queue() with no
bounds check. Reserving those slots means knowing how many queues exist, and
this driver did not: DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES was NR_CPUS, so the same code offered
16 queues under one kernel configuration and 64 under another, sizing
egress_fqs[] and conf_fqs[] to match. A budget that moves with someone else's
Kconfig cannot be part of an interface contract.

Pin the direct queues at sixteen and reserve DPAA_ETH_CEETM_LEAF_QUEUES more
above them, one per CEETM class queue on a channel. Sixteen is not a new
number - it is what the more constrained configuration already ran, so that
Tx path is untouched and the other moves down to a count already in service.
It stays a power of two because the Tx paths index conf_fqs[] with & (N - 1).
The count is not derived from the core count: these queues carry only what the
CPU sends, an offloaded flow reaches none of them, and with no XPS maps here a
queue is picked by flow hash rather than by CPU.

netif_set_real_num_tx_queues() then reports only the direct queues as usable -
the first such call this driver has made - so a leaf class is invisible until
the module that hands one out says otherwise. Nothing allocates an egress FQ
for a reserved slot: a leaf class sends through a CEETM logical FQ that the
scheduler owns.

Add the ndo_setup_tc that hands those slots out, as a handler the offload
module registers, since a built-in driver cannot link against one.

That ndo is load-bearing beyond tc. Netfilter chooses how to bind a hardware
flowtable on whether the netdev has an ndo_setup_tc at all
(nf_flow_table_offload_setup), so from here every such bind arrives through
this callback rather than through the module's indirect registration. With no
handler registered the binds are refused, where previously they were served;
the module registers one at init, and keeps its indirect registration too so
it still works on a kernel without this patch.

Add the data path that makes a leaf class reachable, as a second pair of
callbacks the same module registers. ndo_select_queue asks it which leaf a
frame belongs to, and cpe_fp_tx() then reads the class back out of the queue
index rather than resolving the frame's mark a second time - which is what
stops the software and hardware paths deciding a flow's class independently
and disagreeing. A frame that named no class keeps the stack's own queue
choice, folded back below the leaf range so ordinary traffic cannot land on a
class queue by hashing into it.

Confirm on the queue that sent the frame while here. cpe_fp_tx() indexed
conf_fqs[] with queuenum, which in the CEETM branch is a class-queue id and
not a Tx queue at all: a DSCP-classified frame, whose queuenum is always zero,
confirmed on conf_fqs[0] whichever core sent it. That is a pre-existing defect
rather than a consequence of this work, but the correct index is the one this
patch makes meaningful.

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/sdk_dpaa/dpaa_eth.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/sdk_dpaa/dpaa_eth.h
@@ -194,13 +194,42 @@
 #define fm_l4_frame_is_tcp(parse_result_ptr) \
 	((parse_result_ptr)->l4r & FM_L4_PARSE_RESULT_TCP)
 
-/* number of Tx queues to FMan */
+/* Number of Tx queues to FMan that carry ordinary, unclassified traffic.
+ *
+ * These are the queues the CPU sends through: locally generated traffic, flows
+ * that were never offloaded, and the punt path. A hardware-offloaded flow
+ * never reaches one, so this count says nothing about offload capacity.
+ *
+ * It was NR_CPUS, which is a distribution's build-time ceiling rather than a
+ * property of the board: the same driver offered 16 queues under one kernel
+ * configuration and 64 under another, and sized egress_fqs[] and conf_fqs[] to
+ * match. A hardware qdisc allocates its leaf classes out of the queues above
+ * this count, so a budget that moves with someone else's Kconfig cannot be
+ * part of an interface contract.
+ *
+ * Sixteen is chosen because it is what the more constrained of those two
+ * configurations already ran, which makes this a fixed number rather than a
+ * new one. It stays a power of two because the Tx paths index conf_fqs[] with
+ * & (DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES - 1). Queues are picked by flow hash rather than by
+ * CPU -- this driver installs no XPS maps -- so the count trades qdisc
+ * contention between concurrent software senders against the frame queues each
+ * one costs, and is not bounded by the core count in either direction.
+ */
 #ifdef CONFIG_FMAN_PFC
 #define DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES	(NR_CPUS * CONFIG_FMAN_PFC_COS_COUNT)
 #else
-#define DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES	NR_CPUS
+#define DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES	16
 #endif
 
+/* Headroom above the direct queues, one slot per CEETM class queue on a
+ * channel, reserved so a hardware qdisc can hand sch_htb a real and
+ * exclusively owned netdev_queue index for each leaf class. Nothing allocates
+ * an egress FQ for these: a leaf's frames leave through a CEETM logical FQ
+ * that the scheduler owns, not through egress_fqs[].
+ */
+#define DPAA_ETH_CEETM_LEAF_QUEUES	16
+#define DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES_MAX	(DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES + DPAA_ETH_CEETM_LEAF_QUEUES)
+
 #define DPAA_ETH_RX_QUEUES	128
 #define DPAA_IP_VERSION_4  4
 
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/sdk_dpaa/dpaa_eth_common.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/sdk_dpaa/dpaa_eth_common.h
@@ -111,6 +111,44 @@
 typedef struct qman_fq *(*cdx_get_ceetm_dscp_fq)(void *, uint8_t dscp);
 int dpa_register_ceetm_get_egress_fq(cdx_get_ceetm_egressfq egress_fq_func, cdx_get_ceetm_dscp_fq dscp_fq_func);
 void dpa_unregister_ceetm_get_egress_fq(void);
+
+/* Handler for the netdev's ndo_setup_tc, registered by the offload module
+ * because a built-in driver cannot link against one.
+ *
+ * Registering is not optional once the ndo exists. Netfilter picks its
+ * flowtable binding route on whether the netdev has an ndo_setup_tc at all
+ * (nf_flow_table_offload_setup), so from the moment this driver offers one,
+ * every hardware flowtable bind arrives here instead of through the module's
+ * indirect registration. With no handler those binds are refused, where before
+ * they were served.
+ */
+typedef int (*dpa_setup_tc_handler)(struct net_device *dev,
+				    enum tc_setup_type type, void *type_data);
+int dpa_register_setup_tc(dpa_setup_tc_handler handler);
+void dpa_unregister_setup_tc(void);
+
+/* The data-path half of the hardware qdisc, which only the module that hands
+ * out leaf classes can answer.
+ *
+ * select_queue turns a frame into the Tx queue of the leaf class it belongs
+ * to, or DPA_SELECT_QUEUE_NONE when it belongs to none, which leaves the
+ * stack's own choice in place -- folded below the leaf range by the caller,
+ * because real_num_tx_queues covers those queues once a class is using one and
+ * nothing but a named class may be sent there.
+ *
+ * txq_fq then names the CEETM frame queue behind that Tx queue, or NULL when
+ * the queue is an ordinary one. Reading the class from the queue index rather
+ * than resolving the mark a second time is what keeps the software path on the
+ * same class the hardware path put the flow on.
+ */
+#define DPA_SELECT_QUEUE_NONE	((u16)~0U)
+struct dpa_qdisc_ops {
+	u16 (*select_queue)(struct net_device *dev, struct sk_buff *skb);
+	struct qman_fq *(*txq_fq)(void *qm_ctx, u16 txq);
+};
+int dpa_register_qdisc_ops(const struct dpa_qdisc_ops *ops);
+void dpa_unregister_qdisc_ops(void);
+struct qman_fq *dpa_qdisc_txq_fq(void *qm_ctx, u16 txq);
 #endif
 
 
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/sdk_dpaa/dpaa_eth.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/sdk_dpaa/dpaa_eth.c
@@ -684,6 +684,95 @@
 }
 #endif
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_CPE_FAST_PATH
+/* Set by the offload module; a built-in driver cannot link against one. */
+static dpa_setup_tc_handler dpa_setup_tc_func;
+
+int dpa_register_setup_tc(dpa_setup_tc_handler handler)
+{
+	if (!handler)
+		return -EINVAL;
+	if (cmpxchg(&dpa_setup_tc_func, NULL, handler))
+		return -EBUSY;
+	return 0;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(dpa_register_setup_tc);
+
+void dpa_unregister_setup_tc(void)
+{
+	WRITE_ONCE(dpa_setup_tc_func, NULL);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(dpa_unregister_setup_tc);
+
+/* Read the handler once: an unregister can NULL it concurrently, and a
+ * half-torn read would call through freed module text.
+ *
+ * Refusing when nothing is registered is deliberate rather than convenient.
+ * Netfilter has already chosen this route over the module's indirect
+ * registration by the time it gets here, so answering anything but an error
+ * would claim a binding no one is going to service.
+ */
+static int dpa_setup_tc(struct net_device *dev, enum tc_setup_type type,
+			void *type_data)
+{
+	dpa_setup_tc_handler handler = READ_ONCE(dpa_setup_tc_func);
+
+	return handler ? handler(dev, type, type_data) : -EOPNOTSUPP;
+}
+
+/* The data-path half, published and retired as one pointer so a frame never
+ * sees one callback without the other. */
+static const struct dpa_qdisc_ops *dpa_qdisc;
+
+int dpa_register_qdisc_ops(const struct dpa_qdisc_ops *ops)
+{
+	if (!ops || !ops->select_queue || !ops->txq_fq)
+		return -EINVAL;
+	/* release-publish so a frame cannot see the pointer before the queue
+	 * map it walks is initialized */
+	if (cmpxchg_release(&dpa_qdisc, NULL, ops))
+		return -EBUSY;
+	return 0;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(dpa_register_qdisc_ops);
+
+/* Waits out in-flight Tx before returning, so the callbacks' module text may
+ * be freed once it does. */
+void dpa_unregister_qdisc_ops(void)
+{
+	WRITE_ONCE(dpa_qdisc, NULL);
+	synchronize_net();
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(dpa_unregister_qdisc_ops);
+
+struct qman_fq *dpa_qdisc_txq_fq(void *qm_ctx, u16 txq)
+{
+	const struct dpa_qdisc_ops *ops = READ_ONCE(dpa_qdisc);
+
+	return ops ? ops->txq_fq(qm_ctx, txq) : NULL;
+}
+
+static u16 dpa_qdisc_select_queue(struct net_device *dev, struct sk_buff *skb,
+				  struct net_device *sb_dev)
+{
+	const struct dpa_qdisc_ops *ops = READ_ONCE(dpa_qdisc);
+	u16 queue;
+
+	if (ops) {
+		queue = ops->select_queue(dev, skb);
+		if (queue != DPA_SELECT_QUEUE_NONE)
+			return queue;
+	}
+	/* Traffic that named no class keeps the stack's own choice -- socket
+	 * affinity and XPS included -- folded back into the queues that carry
+	 * it. The fold is what keeps it off the leaf classes above them, which
+	 * real_num_tx_queues now covers and which are reached only by naming
+	 * one; DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES is a power of two for exactly this kind of
+	 * masking. */
+	return netdev_pick_tx(dev, skb, sb_dev) & (DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES - 1);
+}
+#endif
+
 static const struct net_device_ops dpa_private_ops = {
 	.ndo_open = dpa_eth_priv_start,
 	.ndo_start_xmit = dpa_tx,
@@ -692,8 +781,13 @@
 	.ndo_get_stats64 = dpa_get_stats64,
 	.ndo_set_mac_address = dpa_set_mac_address,
 	.ndo_validate_addr = eth_validate_addr,
-#ifdef CONFIG_FMAN_PFC
+#if defined(CONFIG_FMAN_PFC)
 	.ndo_select_queue = dpa_select_queue,
+#elif defined(CONFIG_CPE_FAST_PATH)
+	/* Load-bearing beyond the qdisc: the leaf-class queues live above
+	 * real_num_tx_queues' probe-time value, and once one is in service the
+	 * stack's own hash would otherwise spread ordinary traffic across it. */
+	.ndo_select_queue = dpa_qdisc_select_queue,
 #endif
 	.ndo_set_rx_mode = dpa_set_rx_mode,
 	.ndo_init = dpa_ndo_init,
@@ -703,6 +797,9 @@
 #endif
 	.ndo_set_features = dpa_set_features,
 	.ndo_fix_features = dpa_fix_features,
+#ifdef CONFIG_CPE_FAST_PATH
+	.ndo_setup_tc = dpa_setup_tc,
+#endif
 };
 
 static int dpa_private_napi_add(struct net_device *net_dev)
@@ -779,6 +876,15 @@
 				NETIF_F_RXCSUM;
 	net_dev->lltx               = true;
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_CPE_FAST_PATH
+	/* tc_can_offload() gates every hardware qdisc on this bit, and it has
+	 * to be in features rather than only in hw_features to be on by
+	 * default. Offered alongside ndo_setup_tc, since neither is any use
+	 * without the other. */
+	net_dev->hw_features |= NETIF_F_HW_TC;
+	net_dev->features |= NETIF_F_HW_TC;
+#endif
+
 	/* Advertise S/G and HIGHDMA support for private interfaces */
 	net_dev->hw_features |= NETIF_F_SG | NETIF_F_HIGHDMA;
 	/* Recent kernels enable GSO automatically, if
@@ -953,11 +1059,25 @@
 	/* Allocate this early, so we can store relevant information in
 	 * the private area (needed by 1588 code in dpa_mac_probe)
 	 */
-	net_dev = alloc_etherdev_mq(sizeof(*priv), DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES);
+	/* Allocate the leaf-class headroom up front, the way every other
+	 * hardware-qdisc driver does: sch_htb turns the qid a driver hands back
+	 * straight into netdev_get_tx_queue() with no bounds check, so those
+	 * slots have to exist before a class can name one. Only the direct
+	 * queues are live until then; see the real_num_tx_queues call below. */
+	net_dev = alloc_etherdev_mq(sizeof(*priv), DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES_MAX);
 	if (!net_dev) {
 		dev_err(dev, "alloc_etherdev_mq() failed\n");
 		goto alloc_etherdev_mq_failed;
 	}
+	/* alloc_etherdev_mq() reports every allocated queue as usable. Narrow
+	 * that to the queues that actually have an egress FQ behind them, so
+	 * ordinary traffic never hashes into the reserved range and a later
+	 * qdisc sees an honest count of pre-existing direct queues. */
+	err = netif_set_real_num_tx_queues(net_dev, DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES);
+	if (err) {
+		dev_err(dev, "netif_set_real_num_tx_queues() = %d\n", err);
+		goto netif_set_real_num_tx_queues_failed;
+	}
 
 	/* Do this here, so we can be verbose early */
 	SET_NETDEV_DEV(net_dev, dev);
@@ -1152,6 +1272,8 @@
 alloc_failed:
 mac_probe_failed:
 	dev_set_drvdata(dev, NULL);
+	/* Reached before drvdata is set, so it has nothing to undo. */
+netif_set_real_num_tx_queues_failed:
 	free_netdev(net_dev);
 alloc_etherdev_mq_failed:
 	if (atomic_read(&dpa_bp->refs) == 0)
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/sdk_dpaa/dpaa_eth_sg.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/sdk_dpaa/dpaa_eth_sg.c
@@ -1973,9 +1973,20 @@
 		egress_fq = NULL;
 		get_ceetm_cm_egress_fq = 1; /* This becomes 0 when qosconnark(markval is 0) is not present or
 						can't find egress fq for dscp */
+		/* A hardware qdisc has already decided this frame's class, in
+		 * ndo_select_queue and from the same key the hardware rule used,
+		 * and put the frame on that leaf's Tx queue. Reading the class
+		 * back out of the queue index is what stops the two paths
+		 * resolving it twice and disagreeing. Queues that are not leaf
+		 * classes answer NULL, which is every queue when no qdisc is
+		 * attached, and the mark and DSCP paths below are then reached
+		 * exactly as before. */
+		egress_fq = dpa_qdisc_txq_fq(priv->qm_ctx, skb_get_queue_mapping(skb));
+		if (egress_fq)
+			get_ceetm_cm_egress_fq = 0;
 		/* markval is 0 means qosconnmark is not configured, so getting dscp from skb. */
 		/* using dscp get the egress fq. */
-		if (!markval && ceetm_get_dscp_fq) {
+		if (get_ceetm_cm_egress_fq && !markval && ceetm_get_dscp_fq) {
 			skb_nh = dpa_get_skb_nh(skb, &l3_proto, &l3_offset);
 			if (skb_nh)
 			{
@@ -2017,7 +2028,14 @@
 			queuenum = queuenum % DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES;
 		egress_fq = priv->egress_fqs[queuenum];
 	}
-	conf_fq = priv->conf_fqs[queuenum  & (DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES-1)];
+	/* Confirm on the queue that sent the frame. This indexed conf_fqs[] with
+	 * queuenum, which in the CEETM branch is a class-queue id and not a Tx
+	 * queue at all -- so a DSCP-classified frame, whose queuenum is always
+	 * zero, confirmed on conf_fqs[0] whichever core sent it, and a marked
+	 * one confirmed on whichever conf queue its class happened to number.
+	 * Neither is the queue whose Tx ring the frame left by, which is what
+	 * confirmation accounting and completion affinity both assume. */
+	conf_fq = priv->conf_fqs[skb_get_queue_mapping(skb) & (DPAA_ETH_TX_QUEUES - 1)];
 	return dpa_tx_extended(skb, net_dev, egress_fq, conf_fq);
 }
 #endif
